Using kinematic equation,
$\begin{aligned}
\theta & =\omega_0 \mathrm{t}+\frac{1}{2} \alpha \mathrm{t}^2 \\
\theta & =\frac{1}{2} \alpha \mathrm{t}^2 \\
\therefore \quad \mathrm{t} & =\left(\frac{2 \theta}{\alpha}\right)^{1 / 2}...(i)
\end{aligned}\ldots   \left(\because\right.$ particle was at rest, $\left.\omega_0=0\right)$Angular displacement of the particle $=  \mathrm{r} \theta...($ ii)
$\begin{aligned}
\therefore \quad& \text{ Average velocity }=\frac{\text{ Angular displacement }}{\text{ time }}\\
& \mathrm{V}_{\text{average }}=\frac{\mathrm{ r} \theta}{\ mathrm{t}}=\frac{\mathrm{r} \theta}{\left(\frac{2 \theta}{\alpha}\right)^{1 / 22}}=\frac{\m mathrm{r}}{\sqrt{2}} \sqrt{\alpha \theta}
\end{aligned}$

At end points of the half revolution magnitude of the velocity is same but it directs in opposite direction. $\begin{array}{ll}
\therefore \Delta$ V=V-(-V) \\
\therefore $\Delta$ V=2 V
\end{array} Time taken to complete the half revolution is $\mathrm{t}=\frac{\pi \mathrm{r}}{\mathrm{V}}$ Average acceleration is, $a=\frac{\Delta V}{t}=\frac{2 \mathrm{ V}}{\frac{\pi \mathrm{r}}{\mathrm{V}}}
\therefore \quad a=\frac{2 V^2}{\pi r}$

Torque: $\tau=\mathrm{I} \alpha=\frac{\mathrm{MR}^2}{2} \times \frac{\omega}{\mathrm{t}} \begin{array}{ll}
\therefore& \tau=\frac{\mathrm{MR}^2 \omega}{2 \mathrm{t}}\\
& \text{ But } \tau=\mathrm{R} \times \mathrm{F}\\
\therefore \quad & \mathrm{F}=\frac{\tau}{\mathrm{R}}=\frac{\mathrm{MR} \omega}{2 \mathrm{t}}
\end{array}$

In rotational motion,
$\begin{aligned}
& \omega=\omega_0+\alpha \mathrm{t} \\
& \omega=\alpha \mathrm{t}
\end{aligned}\text{ ( } \because \omega_0=0 \text{; particle at rest.) }\therefore \quad$ Centrifugal acceleration $\mathrm{a}=\omega^2 \mathrm{r}\therefore \quad \mathrm{a}=\alpha^2 \mathrm{t}^2 \mathrm{r}
Tangential acceleration \mathrm{a}_{\mathrm{t}}=\alpha \times \mathrm{r}
Given: \mathrm{a}=\mathrm{a}_{\mathrm{t}$}\Rightarrow \alpha^2 $\mathrm{t}^2 \mathrm{r}=$\alpha $\mathrm{r}\mathrm{t}^2=\frac{1}{\alpha}=\frac{1}{4}$\therefore \quad $\mathrm{t}=\frac{1}{2}=$0.5 $\mathrm{ s}$

$\mathrm{u}_{\mathrm{x}}=\mathrm{a}=$ Horizontal component of the velocity
$\mathrm{u}_{\mathrm{y}}=\mathrm{b}=$ Vertical component of the velocity
Maximum height, $\mathrm{H}=\frac{\mathrm{u}_{\mathrm{y}^2}{2 \mathrm{ g}}=\frac{\mathrm{b}^2}{2 \mathrm{ g}}$
Range, $\mathrm{R}=\frac{2 \mathrm{u}_{\mathrm{y}} \mathrm{u}_{\mathrm{x}}}{\mathrm{g}}=\frac{2 \mathrm{ba}}{\mathrm{g}}
\begin{aligned}$& \because $\mathrm{R}=2 \mathrm{H}\\ \text{$ and $} \therefore \frac{2 \mathrm{ba}}{\mathrm{g}}=\frac{2 \mathrm{ b}^2}{2 \mathrm{ g}}\\ \text{ and } \therefore \mathrm{b}=2 \mathrm{a}
\end{aligned}$

Change in speed after half revolution is $2 \mathrm{ V} \begin{aligned}
\quad& \text{ Time taken }=\frac{\pi R}{V}\\
\therefore \quad \text{ average speed }&=\frac{2 V^{2}}{\pi R}
\end{aligned}$

